[section:release_notes Release Notes]

[heading Unreleased]

* Contexts: `session::basic_http<Context>` creates one context object per
  connection; `handler::rest` callables request it (and any other context
  in scope) through leading `T&`, `T const&` or `shared_ptr<T>` parameters.
* `core::context_store`: thread-safe keyed store with idle expiry,
  per-entry asynchronous locking and minted keys.
  `handler::with_session` binds a store to a cookie or header and injects
  the keyed context into the wrapped handler.
* JSON-RPC 2.0: `jsonrpc::endpoint<Context...>` with named parameters
  (`rpc_arg`), batches, notifications, standard error codes and a
  replaceable error mapper.
* MCP: `mcp::endpoint<Session, Context...>` over Streamable HTTP with
  tools, resources, URI templates, sessions, protocol version negotiation,
  Origin validation, and a server-to-client event stream (`stream_handler`,
  `notify`, `broadcast`).
* OpenAPI: `openapi::generator` renders an OpenAPI 3.1 document from routes
  registered with `describe(...)`; inexpressible matchers are rejected at
  compile time. `register_request_handler` accepts trailing registration
  observers.
* Argument providers accept descriptions; `rest` handlers expose `params()`.
  `response_from` accepts `boost::json::object` and `boost::json::array`.
* `core::async_channel`: executor-agnostic asynchronous queue used for
  server-push streams.
* JSON Schema generation for `boost::describe`d structs and enums,
  sequences and maps (`json_schema_for`), shared by OpenAPI and MCP;
  described types and JSON containers as REST return values.
* REST request providers (`header_arg`, `cookie_arg`, `query_arg`) accepted
  on JSON-RPC methods and MCP tools.
* `mcp::session_info` context; `mcp::endpoint::serve_stdio` transport.
* `core::cors` policy and `basic_http::set_response_filter`.
* Streaming request bodies: `body_reader`, `streaming_request` and the
  `stream_body_arg` provider; the body limit does not apply to them.
* `multipart/form-data`: `parse_multipart` and `multipart_form_arg`
  (buffered), `multipart_reader` and `multipart_stream_arg` (streamed).
* JSON-RPC positional parameters: a `params` array binds to `rpc_arg`
  providers in order.
* `with_session` accepts streaming inner handlers (`chunked_response`,
  `async_generator`); `session_key::*::key_header`.
* `handler::constrained` argument wrapper: declarative validation with
  JSON Schema keywords on REST, JSON-RPC and MCP; `error::constraint_violated`.
* OpenAPI: `default` from `with_default`, security schemes
  (`add_security_scheme`, `set_security`, `operation::security`,
  inferred from credential headers), `components.schemas` holding the
  described types, referenced from operations (`set_inline_schemas` to
  opt out).
* MCP server-to-client requests: `endpoint::request`, `create_message`
  (sampling), `elicit`, `list_roots`; `client_capabilities` and
  `client_info` on `session_info`; `client_error`; `on_client_notification`.
* MCP binary content: `mcp::image`, `audio`, `embedded_resource`,
  `tool_result` and `blob` resources; `base64_encode`/`base64_decode`.
  SSE event ids with `Last-Event-ID` resumption.
* MCP prompts (`prompt()`, `prompts/list`, `prompts/get`), resource
  subscriptions (`resources/subscribe`, `resource_updated`), list-changed
  broadcasts, and `*/list` pagination via `server_info::page_size`.
* `jsonrpc::request_scope`: per-request id, cancellation and notification
  sink injectable into methods and tools; `endpoint::cancel(id)`; MCP
  `notifications/cancelled` and `notifications/progress`.
* `405 Method Not Allowed` with `Allow` for known paths, and `HEAD` served
  from `GET` routes with the body stripped; `set_method_not_allowed` and
  `set_head_from_get` switch them off. `matcher::route_shape` exposes the
  route analysis the OpenAPI generator and the session share.
* TLS: `basic_http` takes its stream type as a template parameter;
  `session::https` and `server::tls` serve HTTPS (OpenSSL required).
* `ROADMAP.md` lists the remaining gaps.
* `session::http`: responses follow the request's HTTP version and
  keep-alive; pipelined requests are served in order; a peer closing an
  idle connection is no longer reported as a hard error; shutdown lets
  in-flight handlers finish and closes afterwards; mid-stream generator
  exceptions reach the hard error handler.
* Test suite extended with user-scenario coverage (dual-surface
  consistency, transport and lifecycle e2e, streaming e2e, routing edge
  cases) and a compile-fail probe harness under `test/compile_fail/`.
* Matcher metadata: every matcher expression carries a compile-time
  description of its constraints and captured values.
* Examples reorganised around use cases (hello, REST CRUD with OpenAPI,
  connection state, cookie sessions, streaming, proxy, WebSocket, JSON-RPC,
  MCP).
* Initial release preparation. Headers relocated to
  `include/boost/taar/` to match the Boost modular layout.
* Added `build.jam`, `meta/libraries.json`, and a `doc/` directory built on
  QuickBook + Doxygen for integration with the Boost documentation build.

[heading 0.0.46]

* `cmake.test()` is used in the Conan recipe instead of a hand-built test
  executable path.

[heading 0.0.44]

* `session::http`: surface mid-stream chunked errors;
  harden `flatten_awaiter`.
* MSVC `/bigobj` for heavy templates and a raw-handler test fix on Windows.

[heading 0.0.43]

* `session::http`: add `register_raw_handler` for protocol upgrades such
  as WebSocket.

[endsect]
